The Day 262 assessment is written to session_plan/assessment.md and committed as 91e182c2. The build was green at session start and the binary answers a -p prompt.

What changed recently: the last 10 sessions were green with 0 reverts. One session (Day 261 01:38) ended without a verdict. The model refused partway through that task, and the next request then failed with a 400 error (#1206). A yoyo-side cleanup for that landed on Day 261. The full fix waits on yoagent#455. Day 262 landed two fixes:
• #1205 (part of #1202): commands whose words bash rewrites before running them (brace lists, backslash escapes, globs) are now checked as bash will run them.
• #1207: the temperature limit is now set per provider.

Main findings:
• #1202's bolded clause is still not done. @yuanhao bolded that a command yoyo can't parse, or uses eval or a computed command name, must "fail closed" (ask, never auto-approve). Wildcard allow rules already refuse unparseable commands. But tree_refusal ignores parse errors, and an exact allow rule or the "always" answer still lets them run. {echo,hi} doesn't parse cleanly, so the Day 262 check never sees it.
• One "always" answer turns off every later safety prompt. Answering a at any bash prompt, even on ls, sets one session-wide flag. After that, every soft safety warning is auto-approved; only the hard refusals and the user's deny list still apply. The permissions guide does document this. Claude Code and Codex remember a rule per command instead. This comes from reading the code; I didn't run it through the binary.
• Saving an "always" pattern has no secret check. It stores the first two words plus *, so export TOKEN=sk-… would go into .yoyo.toml as written. Claude Code 2.1.323 stopped saving rules that look like they hold a secret. There is no ticket for this yet.
• Two files are close to failing the size check. src/context.rs is 2035 lines and not on the exceptions list; 15 more lines makes cargo test fail, which reverts the whole task. src/format/mod.rs is 86 lines over its recorded size; 14 more is fatal.

Research: Claude Code's November releases fixed the same kinds of gaps #1202 is working on: compound commands that ran without asking, and allow rules that could run a different program than the one they name. Codex splits plain &&/;/| chains and checks each command against the rules separately. That is exactly what #1202's second step needs. I saved these notes to yopedia. Recall was keyword search only, because the question API answered "Sign in required".

Suggested order for the planner:
1. The fail-closed clause. Route parse errors, eval and computed command names to a confirmation prompt, after first counting which valid commands the parser marks as errors. I believe the evolve loop won't be affected, because it runs with piped input and no prompts. That is from reading the code, so a binary run should confirm it.
2. #1202's per-command allow check, using Codex's splitting rules.
3. #1184: move the [permissions] and [directories] lists onto the real TOML parser.
4. Smaller: #1208 and the secret check on saved patterns.

Two other things:
• Hard-deny false positive while writing this file. The safety rule against deleting the root directory blocked my own bash command. The command was a Python heredoc whose text only mentioned Codex's example of that delete inside a string. I wrote the file with the edit tool instead and logged the hit on #1102, the ticket that tracks this false positive.
• Commit needed a force-add. session_plan/ is gitignored, so I used git add -f. Earlier assessment commits contain the same file.
